\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281363 1113 #uuid "3ece8891-3519-531e-9799-b3b357c6d488" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281363 1145 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281365 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281366 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281367 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281368 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281369 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1041 "selection" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281366 13194139534190 true] [17592186281364 1110 #uuid "046fffba-77af-5ed3-9357-e6ff4dd88b10" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281365 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534190 true] [17592186281365 1111 #uuid "4ba472f7-f313-51f2-a104-7a19c58b2c77" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281366 1034 "I only" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534190 true] [17592186281366 1111 #uuid "edef8f5b-d98f-5c89-bd5d-61900e8465e3" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281367 1034 "III only" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534190 true] [17592186281367 1111 #uuid "dd36ed9f-899d-58cb-a7ac-30d94879b825" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281368 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534190 true] [17592186281368 1111 #uuid "400cf076-e23e-50f7-b419-a4e527afd22b" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281369 1034 "II only" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534190 true] [17592186281369 1111 #uuid "e5c86c03-e515-520c-b8c6-5a893d8f89a7" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281370 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281371 13194139534190 true] [17592186281370 1089 "q-104051" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281370 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534190 true] [17592186281370 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980965/assets/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281370 1113 #uuid "df1e4f10-7dbe-5216-a5eb-493a05d98b17" 13194139534190 true] [17592186281370 1145 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139534190 true] [17592186281371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281372 13194139534190 true] [17592186281371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281373 13194139534190 true] [17592186281371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281374 13194139534190 true] [17592186281371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281375 13194139534190 true] [17592186281371 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281376 13194139534190 true] [17592186281371 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534